rmutations, and we obtain:\nS(z)\n=\nz +\nW +(z)2\n1 −W +(z) + 2W +(z) −W +(z)2\n(1 −W +(z))2\nN+(z) +\nW −(z)2\n1 −W −(z) +\n2W −(z) −W −(z)2\n\u0011\n(1 −W −(z))2\nN−(z) + Si(z) + SiMult(z)\n\u0010S(z) −z\nz\nhal-00348664, version 1 - 19 Dec 2008\n\u0011\n+QW +(z)\n\u0010\nz S(z) −z\n\u0011\n+ QW −(z)\n\u0010\nz S(z) −z\nz\nz\nSolving this equation leads to the following result:\nTheorem 5.1. The class of pin-permutations has a rational generating function:\nS(z) = z\n8z6 −20z5 −4z4 + 12z3 −9z2 + 6z −1\n8z8 −20z7 + 8z6 + 12z5 −14z4 + 26z3 −19z2 + 8z −1\nThe Taylor expansion of S leads to:\nS(z)\n=\nz + 2z2 + 6z3 + 24z4 + 120z5 + 664z6 + 3596z7 + 19004z8\n+99596z9 + 521420z10 + O(z11)\nNotice that the eight ﬁrst terms are already given in [18].\n31--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHow the eigenvalue region of standardized Laplacians leaves the 2005 polygon (https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.02249)\nciteturn1academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 days ago; Let $Λ_n$ be the set of all eigenvalues of $n\\times n$ standardized Laplacian matrices, and let $S_n\\subseteqΛ_n$ be the polygon introduced in 2005, with upper vertices $λ_0=0,\\dots,λ_{n-1}=1$.\nTitle: How the eigenvalue region of standardized Laplacians leaves the 2005 polygon\nAuthors: Pavel Chebotarev\nDate: Sun Nov  1 22:11:07 2026\n\nLet $Λ_n$ be the set of all eigenvalues of $n\\times n$ standardized Laplacian matrices, and let $S_n\\subseteqΛ_n$ be the polygon introduced in 2005, with upper vertices $λ_0=0,\\dots,λ_{n-1}=1$. It is now known that $Λ_n=S_n$, and that $Λ_n$ is convex, if and only if $n\\le4$. We study how $Λ_n$ leaves $S_n$ near an edge $[λ_m,λ_{m+1}]$, $2(m+1)<n$. First, we prove a switching law for matrices having a boundary point of $Λ_n$ as a simple eigenvalue: an explicit switching function on the arcs, built from the left and right eigenvectors and the outward normal, forces maximal weight where positive and zero weight where negative; intermediate weights occur only where it vanishes. Second, we perturb the weights of the arcs $k\\to k+m+1$ in the circulant digraph (arcs $k\\to k+s$, $1\\le s\\le m$, plus these arcs with weight $τ$) that realizes the point $λ_m+τ(λ_{m+1}-λ_m)$. At second order, the first Fourier harmonic of the perturbation moves the eigenvalue beyond the line of the edge if and only if $τ<τ^*_m=\\sin(πm/n)/(2\\cos(π/n)\\sin(π(m+1)/n))$. For even $n$ and $m$, the point with $τ=τ^*_m$ is the vertex of $S_{n/2}$ lying on this edge, and digraphs with these arcs only at even $k$ give an explicit eigenvalue path that leaves $S_n$ at $λ_m$ and, in all computed cases, ends at this vertex. Third, we give a conjectural, numerically supported picture of $\\partialΛ_5$: beyond each edge of $S_5$ not incident to $0$ or $1$, $Λ_5$ forms a cap bounded by four algebraic arcs, which leaves the edge tangentially at its endpoint adjacent to $0$ or $1$ and rejoins it at $τ^*_1=(3-\\sqrt5)/2$; the rest of the edge lies on $\\partialΛ_5$. In computations for $n\\le8$, a cap returns to its edge smoothly at $τ^*_m$, at a corner at $τ^*_m$, or at a corner beyond $τ^*_m$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEnumeration of Pin-Permutations (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/29614898_Enumeration_of_Pin-Permutations)\nciteturn1search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 17.9 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; tured that the class of pin-permutations has a rational generating function. ... to obtain the generating function of the pin-permutation class in Section 5.\n\n# Enumeration of Pin-Permutations\n\n  * March 2011\n  * The Electronic Journal of Combinatorics 18(1)\n\n\nEnumeration of Pin-Permutations∗\n\nFr´ed´erique Bassino\n\nLIPN, UMR 7030\n\nUniversit´e Paris 13 - CNRS\n\n99, avenue Jean-Baptiste Cl´ement\n\n93430 Villetaneuse, France\n\nMathilde Bouvel\n\nDominique Rossin\n\nLIAFA, Universit´e Paris Diderot\n\nCNRS, UMR 7089, Case 7014\n\n75205 Paris Cedex 13, France\n\nDecember 19, 2008\n\nAbstract\n\nIn this paper, we study the class of pin-permutations, that is to say of permutations\n\nhaving a pin representation. This class has been recently introduced in [16], where it is\n\nused to ﬁnd properties (algebraicity of the generating function, decidability of member-\n\nship) of classes of permutations, depending on the simple permutations this class con-\n\ntains. We give a recursive characterization of the substitution decomposition trees of\n\npin-permutations, which allows us to compute the generating function of this class, and\n\nconsequently to prove, as it is conjectured in [18], the rationality of this generating func-\n\ndecomposition tree with a combinatorial purpose. We deal with permutations that admit\n\npin representations, denoted pin-permutations. These permutations were introduced recently\n\nby Brignall et al. in [16] when studying the links between simple permutations and classes\n\nof pattern avoiding permutations, from an enumerative point of view. The authors conjec-\n\ntured that the class of pin-permutations has a rational generating function. We prove this\n\nconjecture, focusing on the substitution decomposition trees of pin-permutations.\n\nIn Section 2, we start with recalling the deﬁnitions of substitution decomposition and of\n\npin-permutations, and describe some of their basic properties. The core of this work is the\n\nproof of Theorem 3.1 which gives a complete characterization of the decomposition trees of\n\npin-permutations. This corresponds to Section 3. Section 4 focuses on the enumeration of\n\nsimple pin-permutations, using the notion of pin words deﬁned in [18]. With this enumerative\n\nresult and the characterization of Theorem 3.1, standard enumerative techniques [24] allow us\n\nto obtain the generating function of the pin-permutation class in Section 5. This generating\n\nWe call\n\npin-permutation any permutation that has a pin representation. Pin-permutations correspond\n\nto the permutations that can be encoded by pin words in the terminology of [16, 18]. In that\n\npaper the authors conjecture the following result:\n\nConjecture 2.1. [18] The class of pin-permutations has a rational generating function.\n\nIn the sequel we prove this conjecture and exhibit the generating function of pin-permutations.\n\nWe ﬁrst study some properties of pin representations.\n\n2.3 Some properties of pin representations\n\nWe ﬁrst give general properties of pin representations and deﬁne special families of pin-\n\npermutations.\n\n6\n\nhal-00348664, version 1 - 19 Dec 2008\n\nLemma 2.1. Let (p 1, . . . , p n)be a pin representation of σ∈S n. Then for each i∈ {2, . . . , n−\n\n1}, if there exists a point x on the sides of the bounding box of {p 1, . . . , p i}, then it is unique\n\nand x=p i+1.\n\nProof. Consider the bounding box of {p 1, p 2, . . . , p i}and let x be a point on the sides of\n\nthis bounding box. \nbeled by an ascending (resp. descending) quasi-weaving permutation with ﬁxed main\n\nand auxiliary substitution points (enumerated by QW (z) deﬁned in Equation 1) and\n\nsuch that:\n\n–in the main substitution point, we replace the leaf by the tree of a permutation in\n\nS\\ { }. This corresponds to the multiplication by S(z)−z\n\nz, and\n\n–in the auxiliary substitution point, we replace the leaf by 12 (resp. 21). It corre-\n\nsponds to the multiplication by z.\n\nThus we obtain that the generating functions for terms of the above shapes are\n\nQW +(z)z S(z)−z\n\nzand QW −(z)z S(z)−z\n\nz.\n\nWe can ﬁnally rewrite the equation for S into an equation for the generating function\n\nS(z) of pin-permutations, and we obtain:\n\nS(z) = z+W+(z)2\n\n1−W+(z)+2 W+(z)−W+(z)2\n\n(1 −W+(z))2 N+(z) + W−(z)2\n\n1−W−(z)+\n\n2 W−(z)−W−(z)2\n\n(1 −W−(z))2 N−(z) + Si(z) + SiM ult(z)S(z)−z\n\nz\n\n+QW +(z)z S(z)−z\n\nz+QW −(z)z S(z)−z\n\nz\n\nSolving this equation leads to the following result:\n\nTheorem 5.1. The class of pin-permutations has a rational generating function:\n\nThe second point then comes\n\neasily from the deﬁnition of the basis of a permutation class.\n\nThis allows us to conclude that:\n\nTheorem 6.1. The pin-permutation class has an inﬁnite basis.\n\nClasses of permutations having both an inﬁnite basis and a rational generating function are\n\npretty rare in the literature. We found only one example in [1]: the classes T k of permutations\n\nobtained after k transposition switches in series, for k≥5. We can notice that in [1] the\n\nrationality of the generating functions is obtained with automata-theoretic techniques, and\n\nthis can be compared to our proof of Theorem 5.1 where the language of pin words plays a\n\nkey role.\n\n33\n\nhal-00348664, version 1 - 19 Dec 2008\n\n7 Conclusion and open questions\n\nBefore turning back to the original motivations of their deﬁnition, we summarize the im-\n\nprovements that we obtained in the study of pin-permutations. Theorem 3.1 characterizes\n\nthe decomposition trees of pin-permutations, but most importantly it gives a recursive de-\n\nscription of these permutations. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDigraph Laplacian spectra: Counterexamples to the 2005 polygon conjecture (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14100)\nciteturn1academia17 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Title: Digraph Laplacian spectra: Counterexamples to the 2005 polygon conjecture ... Let $Λ_n$ be the set of all eigenvalues of standardized Laplacian matrices of order $n$.\nTitle: Digraph Laplacian spectra: Counterexamples to the 2005 polygon conjecture\nAuthors: Pavel Chebotarev\nDate: Thu Oct  8 20:08:56 2026\n\nLet $Λ_n$ be the set of all eigenvalues of standardized Laplacian matrices of order $n$. In 2005, a polygon $S_n\\subseteqΛ_n$ was constructed, and it was conjectured that $Λ_n=S_n$. We observe that $S_n$ is a zonotope, which gives a short proof of $S_n\\subseteqΛ_n$, and we describe $Λ_n$ as a union, over all vectors $v$, of intersections of zonotopes $Z_i(v)$ built from the ratios $v_j/v_i$. For a vector whose components are the $n$ distinct $n$th roots of unity, every $Z_i(v)$ equals $S_n$. We then show that the conjecture is false for $n=7$: the directed $6$-cycle with one vertex duplicated has an eigenvalue lying $2.1\\cdot10^{-4}$ beyond one edge of $S_7$. For the analogous digraph of order $n$, the corresponding eigenvalue lies at signed distance $δ_n\\sim\\frac{π^2}{2n^4}$ beyond the supporting line of the analogous edge of $S_n$, so the construction yields counterexamples for all sufficiently large $n$; numerically, it does so for every $7\\le n\\le300$. We also prove that $S_m\\subseteq S_n$ whenever $m\\mid n$, with $S_m\\setminus\\{0,1\\}$ lying in the interior of $S_n$ for $n\\ge3m$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOn of the spectrum of the digraph Laplacian (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/251698770_On_of_the_spectrum_of_the_digraph_Laplacian)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 22.9 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; The polygon of Laplacian eigenvalues ... above, the order n of standardized Laplacian matrices is ﬁxed. ... If the conjecture is true, then, by Theorem 6, λ is an eigenvalue of some standard-\nBy Theorem 5,forevery n = 2, 3,..., z(n) \n\n1\n\n2 n\n\ncot\n\nπ\n\n2 n\n\n.\n\nProposition 2. If n is odd, then\n\nz(n) =\n\n1\n\n2 n\n\ncot\n\nπ\n\n2 n\n\n, (23)\n\nmoreover, z(n) = Im(λ\n\n(n−1)/2\n\n), where λ\n\n(n−1)/2\n\nis deﬁned by (19).\n\nThe proposition is proved by substituting k = (n − 1)/2in(21).\n\nIf n>2 is even, then the upper bound\n\n1\n\n2 n\n\ncot\n\nπ\n\n2 n\n\nis not reached by the imaginary\n\nparts of the vertices of S; more speciﬁcally, the following proposition is easy to\n\nverify.\n\nProposition 3. If n>2 is even, then\n\nmax\n\n0k2 n−1\n\nIm(λ\n\nk\n\n) = Im(λ\n\nn/2\n\n) =\n\n1\n\nn\n\ncot\n\nπ\n\nn\n\n<\n\n1\n\n2 n\n\ncot\n\nπ\n\n2 n\n\n.\n\nThe following conjecture is yet unproved.\n\nConjecture 1. All eigenvalues of standardized Laplacian matrices of order n belong\n\nto the polygon S.\n\nIf the conjecture is true, then, by Theorem 6, λ is an eigenvalue of some standard-\n\nized Laplacian matrices of order n if and only if λ ∈ S.\n\nR. Agaev, P. Chebotarev / Linear Algebra and its Applications 399 (2005) 157–168 167\n\n5. Some asymptotic properties of the Laplacian spectra\n\nTheorem 7. The boundary of the polygon S converges, as n →∞, to the curve\n\nmade up by the parts of two cycloids whose parametric equations are:z(τ ) = x(τ) +\n\ni y(τ) and z(τ ) = x(τ) − i y(τ), where\n\nx(τ)=(2 π)\n\n−1\n\n(τ − sin τ), (24)\n\ny(τ)=(2 π)\n\n−1\n\n(1 − cos τ), (25)\n\nand τ ∈[0, 2 π ].\n\nProof. For each τ ∈[0, 2 π), consider any positive integer sequence k(n) such that\n\nlim\n\nn→∞\n\nk(n)\n\nn\n\n=\n\nτ\n\n2 π\n\n. Then, by virtue of (21),\n\nlim\n\nn→∞\n\nλ\n\nk(n)\n\n=\n\n1\n\n2 π\n\n\n\nτ − 2sin\n\nτ\n\n2\n\ncos\n\nτ\n\n2\n\n+ 2i sin\n\n2\n\nτ\n\n2\n\n\n\n=\n\n1\n\n2 π\n\n(τ − sin τ + i(1 − cos τ )).\n\nTaking the real and imaginary parts of this expression, we obtain the desired state-\n\nment. \n\nFig. 3 shows the polygons S at n = 4and n = 5, along with the limit curve spec-\n\niﬁed in Theorem 7.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPin Classes I: Growth Rates and Bounds (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/386502586_Pin_Classes_I_Growth_Rates_and_Bounds)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.9 years ago; Crawled: 4 months ago; this paper, we study the permutation classes that comprise all the ﬁnite subpermutations ... the class of all pin sequences has a rational generating function and established its growth ... has rational generating function and growth rate ω∞«5.24112.\n\n1 Introduction\n\nSince their introduction by Brignall, Huczynska and Vatter [8], pin sequences have proven to\n\nbe objects of considerable interest in the study of permutation classes, especially in connection\n\nwith simple permutations and inﬁnite antichains. Bassino, Bouvel and Rossin [2] showed that\n\nthe class of all pin sequences has a rational generating function and established its growth\n\nrate, and recently, Brignall and Vatter [7] used pin sequences to construct uncountably many\n\nwell-quasi-ordered permutation classes with distinct enumeration sequences.\n\nGiven an (inﬁnite) pin sequence, the pin class is formed from all ﬁnite permutations that are\n\ncontained in the sequence. In this paper we take up the study of pin classes in a more systematic\n\nmanner than has previously been attempted. Our main result is that pin classes have proper\n\ngrowth rates, not merely upper growth rates as guaranteed by the Marcus-Tardos Theorem [12].\n\nWe also describe a procedure for ﬁnding the growth rate of a given pin class in terms of the\n‘-decomposable. This will be important in our later structure theorem for a pin class.\n\nWe denote the class consisting of all centred pin permutations P˝, and its uncentred counter-\n\npart by P. We call this the complete pin class. Bassino, Bouvel and Rossin [2] proved that P\n\n14\n\nhas rational generating function and growth rate ω∞«5.24112. We shall later ﬁnd the gener-\n\nating function of P˝, which of course has the same growth rate as P by Proposition 2.8. We are\n\nprimarily interested in this paper in certain subclasses of P, known as pin classes. These are\n\ndeﬁned somewhat analogously to pin permutations: just as we can convert a (ﬁnite) pin word\n\ninto a permutation, we can also convert an (inﬁnite) pin sequence into a pin class:\n\nDeﬁnition 3.4 (Pin Classes).Suppose that w is an inﬁnite pin sequence. Let π˝\n\nn be the pin per-\n\nmutation obtained by the above process from the inital contiguous subsequence of w of length\n\nn(which is itself a ﬁnite pin word), and consider the set Π=t π˝\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEigenbasis f